Q1. Explain the concept of Data Abstraction in OOP. How does the use of getter and setter methods relate to this principle?

Data Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features of an object to the user.

Getter and Setter Methods
Getter and setter methods help achieve abstraction by providing controlled access to private data.
EXAMPLE-
class Student {
    private String name;

    public void setName(String name) {  //setname
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() {        //getname
        return name;
    }
}

Q2. Describe the scope and lifetime difference between a variable declared inside a for loop and a variable declared outside but within the method block.

for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
Scope: Only inside the for loop.
Lifetime: Exists only while the loop is executing.
Cannot be accessed outside the loop.


Variable outside method 
void display() {
    int x = 10;

    for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        System.out.println(i);
    }

    System.out.println(x); // Valid
}
Scope: From its declaration point to the end of the method block.
Lifetime: Exists for the entire execution of the method.
Can be used anywhere in the method after declaration.

Q3. What is the purpose of the static keyword when applied to a variable or a method? Why can a static method not access non-static instance variables?
A static variable belongs to the class, not to individual objects.

class Student {
    static String college = "MNNIT";
}
Only one copy is created. Shared by all objects.

Why Can't a Static Method Access Non-Static Variables?

A static method cannot directly access non-static variables because non-static variables belong to specific objects, 
while static methods do not have an associated object

Q4. Demonstrate the concept of Method Overloading in Java.
Provide a rule that must be strictly followed for the compiler to differentiate between overloaded methods.

Method Overloading occurs when multiple methods have the same name but different parameter lists in the same class.
EXAMPLE- 
class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));
        System.out.println(c.add(2.5, 3.5));
    }
}
Rule for Method Overloading
The compiler differentiates overloaded methods based on the method signature, which must differ in:

Number of parameters, or
Type of parameters, or
Order of parameter types








